{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General level bias: model is typically ~0.5–2.0 EUR/t too low during sustained uptrends/rallies (early horizon especially). Apply an upward level shift when the last 5–10 days show a clear rising regime.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: beyond ~15–20 days the model tends to fade/mean-revert too aggressively and can undershoot continued strength. Reduce mean-reversion strength at longer horizons in trending markets.",
    "Turning-point lag: after local bottoms the model reacts late (forecasts stay near prior level while realized prices step up). Use momentum/acceleration from the last 3–7 days to steepen the first 1–10 day path.",
    "Overshoot after spikes (late horizon): when the model projects a continued surge to new highs (e.g., >70) it often overshoots and reality consolidates. Cap incremental gains when forecast implies >~2 EUR/t per week without confirming momentum.",
    "Downside overreaction risk: in some runs the model predicts sharp late-horizon drops (mid-60s → low-60s) that do not materialize (realized stays mid/high-60s). Impose a floor based on recent 20–30d support (e.g., do not forecast >~3–4 EUR/t below the 20d median unless recent returns are negative).",
    "Volatility smoothing: realized paths are smoother than forecasted jagged swings. Apply mild smoothing to day-to-day forecast changes (penalize alternating up/down moves) while preserving trend direction."
  ]
}
